// Resolving a specifier to a key — and the one mechanism that answers three separate proposals.
//
// `../README.md` was written without any code in it, because the two import proposals it checks are
// about the *resolver* and the resolver is `packages/wacc/src/path.wac`, which this directory does
// not rewrite. This file exists because a third question arrived on the same day and turns out to
// have the same answer.
//
// ## The three
//
//   1. **A package entry point.** `@/packages/http` rather than `@/packages/http/src/http.wac`.
//   2. **A git dependency.** Already solved, and the way it is solved is the point.
//   3. **An overlay.** `vision/`'s seventeen imports name twelve files that are not in `vision/` and
//      are in `packages/` at the same path — `../../../wac.json5` and `../../../QUESTIONS.md`.
//
// The first and third look like manifest fields and the resolver cannot read a manifest. It cannot
// read anything: `resolveFromAt(fromPath, spec, root, base)` is four strings in and one out, and
// `Res.empty()` is documented as *"what every caller without a filesystem passes"*.
//
// **The second is already a mapping the reader computes**, and `resolveVia` checks mappings first,
// with the reason at the line: *"A mapping first, because only the reader could have resolved one."*
// A git dependency is looked up in a lockfile by something that has a filesystem and handed to the
// resolver as an answer.
//
// So all three are the same shape. The reader knows the layout; the resolver does arithmetic.

import { Vec } from "core";

/**
 * One thing the reader worked out that arithmetic could not.
 *
 * **A value, where the shipped `Res` has three parallel arrays** — `mapFrom`, `mapSpec`, `mapTo`,
 * keyed by position. That is the shape `@/packages/webrtc` found five of in one struct, and the
 * argument is the same: entry `i` of three arrays is one fact, and every site that adds or removes
 * one has to touch all three in the same order.
 *
 * Three is where it is cheapest to fix and easiest to leave.
 */
export struct Mapping {
  /** The importing file's key. */
  string from;
  /** The specifier as written. */
  string spec;
  /** The key it resolves to. */
  string to;
}

/**
 * What the reader knows.
 *
 * `roots` and `base` are unchanged from the shipped `Res`; the mappings are one list.
 */
export struct Known {
  Vec<string> roots;
  Vec<Mapping> mappings;
  /** The directory every relative key is measured from. Empty for a caller with no filesystem. */
  string base;

  /** Nothing known — every caller without a filesystem. */
  Known empty() { … }

  /** The key for `spec` written in `from`, or nothing if arithmetic has to do it. */
  string? mapped(const this, string from, string spec) { … }
}

/**
 * `spec`, as written in `from`, resolved to a key.
 *
 * A mapping first, then arithmetic. Unchanged in behaviour from `resolveFromAt`; what changes is
 * that the three cases above are *visibly* one case.
 */
export string resolve(Known known, string from, string spec) {
  string? m = known.mapped(from, spec);
  if (m !is null) { return m!; }
  return arithmetic(from, spec, known.base);
}

/** `.` and `..` collapsed, `@/` against a root. String arithmetic, no filesystem. */
string arithmetic(string from, string spec, string base) { … }

// ## 1. The overlay needs no language change, and this file is how I found that out
//
// `../../../QUESTIONS.md` says of `vision/`'s seventeen out-of-directory imports that an overlay
// *"is not something the resolver can express"*, and that entry was written before this file. It is
// half right and the half that is wrong matters.
//
// **An overlay is not a manifest key** — that part stands. A key would have to mean *if absent, look
// over there*, and absence is a filesystem question the resolver cannot ask.
//
// **But the reader can ask it**, and handing the answer over is exactly what `Mapping` is for. An
// overlay is: for each import, try `vision/<path>`; if it is not there, use `<path>`; emit a
// mapping. Seventeen mappings for this directory, computed by something that already walks the
// files to read them. No new concept, no manifest field, and the same mechanism a git dependency
// uses — which is the argument, because a git dependency is a *file somewhere else that this
// specifier means*, and so is an overlay.
//
// What is left of the question is smaller and sharper: **should the fallback be the reader's policy
// or something a project can declare?** As a policy it is one flag on whatever drives the compiler
// and no language surface at all. As a declaration it is a manifest field the *reader* honours —
// `{ overlay: ".." }` — which is still not the resolver reading a manifest, and is how a fork or a
// vendored dependency with local changes would want to say it once rather than per invocation.
//
// I recorded the opposite this morning. Writing the resolver is what showed it, which is the same
// lesson as the last four entries and is getting hard to miss.
//
// ## 2. And the entry point is the same mapping, which `../README.md` already worked out
//
// > The proposal should say "a mapping" rather than "a convention", and it did not, because it was
// > written without looking.
//
// Three proposals, one mechanism, and the mechanism exists. What none of the three needs is a change
// to `path.wac`, which is the file everyone assumed they were about.
//
// ## 3. What could not be written
//
// **`Known.mapped` is a linear scan and the shipped one is too.** Three parallel arrays or one list
// of structs, the lookup is *walk until `from` and `spec` both match*. A `Map<string, string>` keyed
// on the pair needs a **two-part key**, and `../../../DECISIONS.md` says references are comparable
// but not hashable, so the key has to be built out of the two strings.
//
// A concatenation with `\0` works — no path contains one — and that is the answer, which is worth
// saying because the first draft of this note said *there is no such character* and there plainly
// is. What is actually missing is smaller: **the key is then a string a caller can build wrongly**,
// by forgetting the separator or choosing a different one, and nothing relates
// `key(from, spec)` to the map it is for. A `Map<K, V>` whose `K` is a struct would, and that is
// hashing a reference, which the decision declines for a stated reason.
//
// One package wants this, this one. `@/packages/wac/src/buildcache.wac` composes a key from four
// things and gets away with it because it **hashes** them — *"the sources, path and text together,
// which is what the key eats a pair at a time"* — so the composite is lossy and a lookup that is
// occasionally wrong is a cache miss. Here it is a resolution, and a wrong answer is a wrong file.
// That is the distinction, and it is more useful than a count: **a composite key is cheap when the
// lookup is allowed to be wrong.**
//
// **Nothing distinguishes *no mapping* from *mapped to nothing*.** `mapped` answers `string?` and
// the shipped one answers `""`, which is the sentinel-in-the-value's-own-range shape again — an
// empty key is not a real key, so it works, and it works for the same accidental reason every one of
// those does. `string?` is right and it makes `resolve` a null check rather than a comparison
// against a value that means two things.
//
// **A mapping is keyed by the importing file, so a file that is reached two ways needs two.** The
// shipped `resolveFromAt`'s whole reason for collapsing `.` and `..` is that *"the same file reached
// two ways would otherwise be two entries in `paths`, and every type it declares would exist
// twice"* — and a mapping's `from` is a key, so it inherits that and nothing states it. For an
// overlay it does not bite, since the mapping is per *specifier occurrence*. For a git dependency
// resolved once and reused by many files, it means one dependency is N mappings.
